        "login": "randomstuff"
      },
      "authorAssociation": "COLLABORATOR",
      "body": "See discussion from https://github.com/OWASP/ASVS/issues/2253#issuecomment-2458046371\r\n\r\n> > Even if you use a random session id it should be in a abstraction like a reference token that is signed or mac'ed to session id tamper requests are not possible.\r\n>\r\n> Is it really necessary to sign or MAC the random token? If so, do we explicitly have this requirement?\r\n> \r\n> Is it enough to use a random token and only store a secure hash of the random token in database? One benefit is that we don't have to manage (rotate, encrypt, etc.) a secret to MAC/sign the token.\r\n",
